# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This guide is for Integration Hub technical team members configuring or
investigating actions and notifications for files in the clean bucket.

## Service behaviour

The file-action adapter finds the most specific dispatch configuration for the
destination object key. It publishes at most one
`FileActionExecutionRequested.v1` event for that configuration. This requests an
action or notification; it does not confirm that either has completed.

Only clean `FileRouted.v1` events for the configured clean bucket reach the
adapter. Quarantine and investigation routes do not produce action requests.

## Check the dispatch configuration

Terraform creates dispatch secrets from `file_dispatch_prefixes` in
[`locals-dispatch.tf`](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/blob/main/terraform/environments/integration-hub-file-transfer/locals-dispatch.tf).
The identity and configured prefix together identify the start of the full
object key. For example, identity `example-transfer-identity` and prefix
`/app-1/` match keys beneath `example-transfer-identity/app-1/`.

Each secret contains an optional action and notification destinations:

```json
{
  "action": {
    "name": "place-on-sqs",
    "queueArn": "arn:aws:sqs:eu-west-2:123456789012:destination"
  },
  "notifications": {
    "email": "consumer@example.invalid",
    "slack": null,
    "teams": "https://example.invalid/teams-webhook"
  }
}
```

This example shows the secret's structure. Before using an action, check that
the service which carries it out (the executor) is deployed. Action-specific
configuration and routing rules are defined when executors are introduced.

`action` is either `null` or an object with a non-empty `name`. Other action
properties contain private configuration used by the selected executor. Each
notification is either `null` or its destination value. A `null` value means
that the action or notification is not configured.

The requested event contains only `action.name`, the names of configured
notification types and a reference to the exact secret version used.
Private action configuration and notification destinations stay in Secrets
Manager. A secret can configure notifications without an action. If neither an
action nor a notification destination is configured, processing succeeds
without publishing an event.

## Check prefix matching

Use the destination object's full key to check which configuration applies.
The adapter first looks for a secret matching the complete key, then tries each
parent prefix from longest to shortest. For `identity/group/reports/test.csv`,
it tries:

1. `identity/group/reports/test.csv`
2. `identity/group/reports/`
3. `identity/group/`
4. `identity/`

The adapter uses the first secret it finds, even if that secret has no action
or notification configured. If a secret does not exist, it tries the next
prefix. If no secret matches, processing succeeds without publishing an event.

If the matching secret cannot be read or has an invalid structure, the
invocation fails. Lambda retries and dead-letter handling apply. The adapter
does not try a less-specific configuration.

## Check delivery and duplicate requests

The adapter uses the route event's `detail.metadata.idempotencyKey` to recognise
requests it has already processed. Replaying the same route with a different
EventBridge event ID does not publish another request while the adapter's
idempotency record exists.

`detail.data.actionExecutionId` is a deterministic UUID derived from the source
route idempotency key, matched secret ARN and exact secret version ID. The output
idempotency key is `action-request:{actionExecutionId}`.

When the adapter processes a different configuration version, it produces a
different execution ID. Changing a secret alone does not bypass the adapter's
existing idempotency record.

The request retains the original `fileId` and `correlationId`, with the
`FileRouted.v1` EventBridge ID as `causationId`. EventBridge publication is at
least once: a retry can publish the same request with a different EventBridge
event ID. Do not use the EventBridge event ID to identify duplicate requests.

Rules can route requests using `detail.data.action.name` or names in
`detail.data.notifications`. One request can be delivered to multiple downstream
queues. Each consumer must use `actionExecutionId` or the equivalent
`detail.metadata.idempotencyKey` to avoid processing the same request twice.

## Protect secret values

The adapter relies on its EventBridge rule to select canonical `FileRouted.v1`
events. It validates the matching secret's JSON structure and action name.
Downstream executors read the action-specific configuration from the exact
secret version identified by `configurationReference.secretArn` and
`configurationReference.secretVersionId` in the event's `detail.data`.

Do not write secret values to EventBridge, CloudWatch Logs or dead-letter
queues. Adapter logs contain only non-sensitive object identifiers,
secret-version identifiers, the action name and configured notification type
names.

For help investigating a failed action request or replaying an event, see
[Investigating file transfer service issues](investigating-file-transfer-service-issues.html)
and [Investigating and replaying file transfer DLQ messages](investigating-and-replaying-file-transfer-dlq-messages.html).